many of you have written to me asking me  to talk about the situation  going on in the country colombia in  south america  and i want to start with that today the  new york times is a good piece  from yesterday updated this morning  summarizing what is going on there there  are now at least  19 people who have been killed in what  are being called pandemic related  protests but more specifically the  protests are because there's been a tax  overhaul connected to the pandemic which  has been proposed  that has turned into anger  from colombians about poverty about  inequality about economic conditions and  indeed as the new york times now  times now reports at least 19 people  have been killed  hundreds have been injured during days  of continued protests in colombia  tens of thousands of people have taken  to the streets to demonstrate  at at least proximally against a tax  overhaul  which uh is as the government claims  necessitated by  a financial whole created  by the pandemic and the president yvonne  duke announced he would withdraw the  current proposal  and put together a new plan saying that  this is a necessity for the country  monday the country's finance minister  announced that he would be resigning but  the protests continue and the repression  continues  and now it is becoming a larger protest  about  poverty in colombia about uh  unemployment in colombia  about inequality in colombia all of  which has been exacerbated by  the pandemic as we've seen in in many  countries  and i've talked a little bit about  what's going on in argentina my country  of birth  incredible anything you can imagine  related to the pandemic has happened in  argentina  officials with vaccines in the trunks of  their car  their cars continued  inflation out of control uh  overwhelmed medical systems strict  lockdowns on paper  that are actually not being followed in  particular by the countries i don't  really want to use the term elite but  upper middle class and many in the  professional class just living as if  there is no pandemic so  there's many examples brazil we have  talked about but this really sort of  taking on a unique shape and character  in colombia and the protests in part are  continuing  because even though the economic  proposal  has been pulled and will be replaced  with something else  the response from the state  has been extremely heavy-handed to put  it lightly and there have been instances  of police abuse captured on video  i'm not going to play them because we'll  just create problems for ourselves  one of one of the unfortunate realities  this is an aside but i think a relevant  one  one of the realities that we live with  in the united states  is that it has for decades  generally speaking not been a  part of protocol to show the violence  that takes place in war  when there is law enforcement repression  and so we in the united states have a  much more  sanitized view of what these  via what this violence looks like around  the world  and as a result of that the policy is  that  if we include a lot of the the violent  footage  it's not just about demonetization our  videos can actually get blocked all  together from from different platforms  and so  the video is out there if you want to go  look for it we won't be including it in  this  um broadcast but there are all sorts of  videos  of police brutality video where young  pro protesters  are seen shot by police  there is an instance of a 17 year old  marcelo agredo who is a ninth grader  sent out to march with his brother shot  by police  died uh after kicking a police officer  going by on a motorbike and uh now we  are seeing  um a sort of uh propaganda battle that's  also taking place  between those who say the police and the  military are now  in danger and they need to be able to  defend themselves a sort of tacit  defense really of the violence against  many of these protesters and of course  those saying  this is a harebrained idea  from the government in proposing this  economic reform  and then now that very same government  because there has been an outcry against  the proposal that they made are um  repressing protesters and um  all sorts of video that has been removed  from social media  already and all of the things that we  often associate with these circumstances  in latin america  are taking place now the there are  numbers that have started coming out i  mentioned already  uh tens of thousands of protesters and  certainly hundreds of injuries the  counter narrative is well 540 police  officers have also been hurt  and there have been endless instances of  property damage  and police say we've identified 17 000  people who have broken the public health  measures because they were protesting  without wearing masks so there is always  a counter narrative but the big picture  here is that  much like many other countries a  a financial shortfall the proposal that  was made economically would have  increased tariffs  on all sorts of everyday goods and  services which would make them  even less affordable for much of the  colombian people  and that caused an outcry and then now  the protesters are get  getting uh knocked knocked around so  we're going to continue following the  situation  all sorts of different uh colombian news  people  and musicians and actors that i follow  on social media have been actively  posting about this and  not enough people in the united states  even know that this is going on at this  point so that's the latest  and these numbers 19 dead there's a  reason why it's  at least 19 dead because in all  likelihood that number is going to go up  tragically so  and we will have more about this maybe  tomorrow maybe friday and  certainly by early next week one of our  sponsors today is  covid rescue fund covid rescue fund is  helping  small business owners who have lost  their businesses during covid  there are over 100 000 small businesses  that have had to close their doors  since the pandemic began there are many  organizations out there  helping businesses who are still open to  stay afloat  but covid rescue fund is one of the few  organizations working to help businesses  reopen who have had to close covet  rescue fund  crowdsources funds to provide grants up  to one hundred thousand dollars  for these owners to create new  businesses  i would implore anyone in my audience to  help covet rescue fund in their mission  by donating at covidrescufund.org  all money that you give is going to the  mission  not to compensating people who work for  the organization  the founders of covid rescue fund are  big fans of the david pakman show  let's help them bring back as many small  businesses as we can  the link is right underneath this video
 

